<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Long March</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Long March was the retreat of the Chinese Communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> from its bases in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1934–35, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>'s fifth encirclement campaign. The Central Red Army left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> in October 1934 and marched some 10,000 kilometres through western China, losing most of its men, before reaching northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> in October 1935. During the march, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zunyi">Zunyi</a> Conference of January 1935, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> rose to the leadership of the party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/long-march.jpg" alt="Long March" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chinese_civil_war_map_03.jpg: User:Guimard derivative work: Rowanwindwhistler (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Long_March_1934-1935-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
